// ATCV Self-Test — Part 2 (L12-L18 question data)
window.S_PART_2 = [{"id": "a1", "ch": "l1213", "g": "l1213-core", "ct": "Lectures 12–13", "cs": "MLPs and Introduction to CNNs", "sc": null, "tit": "The Paradigm Shift", "b": "L12–13 · §01", "q": [{"p": "What was the standard image-classification pipeline before 2012?", "o": ["End-to-end CNN trained on raw pixels", "SIFT/HOG features → Bag-of-Visual-Words → SVM", "Vision Transformer with patch embeddings", "GAN-based unsupervised features + k-NN"], "c": 1, "e": "Pre-2012, only the SVM was learned. SIFT/HOG features were prescriptive — designed by humans, not learned from data.", "tag": null}, {"p": "Which two enablers triggered the 2012 deep-learning revolution?", "o": ["Cloud TPUs and crowdsourced labels", "ImageNet (~1.2M labelled images) and GPGPU / CUDA", "Quantum compute and synthetic data", "Transformer attention and SSDs"], "c": 1, "e": "Both were necessary. ImageNet supplied the scale, and CUDA-enabled GPUs made convolution affordable. Either alone would not have been enough.", "tag": null}, {"p": "Approximately by how much did AlexNet cut the ImageNet top-5 error?", "o": ["From 50% → 30%", "From 10% → 5%", "From 26% → 16%", "From 16% → 8%"], "c": 2, "e": "AlexNet (NeurIPS 2012) dropped top-5 error from ~26% to ~16% — the defining moment for deep learning.", "tag": null}, {"p": "Why is end-to-end learning fundamentally more powerful than hand-crafted features?", "o": ["It runs faster at inference time", "It requires no GPU", "The loss gradient shapes every stage to fit the task; hand-crafted features have a fixed expressive ceiling", "It avoids backpropagation altogether"], "c": 2, "e": "End-to-end training jointly optimises features, representation, and classifier toward the actual task. Hand-crafted features are bounded by their human-designed structure.", "tag": null}, {"p": "In the classical SIFT → BoVW → SVM pipeline, which component was actually learned?", "o": ["The SIFT features", "The BoVW codebook", "The SVM classifier", "Everything"], "c": 2, "e": "SIFT is a fixed algorithm. The BoVW codebook is typically k-means (unsupervised). Only the SVM is discriminatively trained on labelled data.", "tag": null}, {"p": "AlexNet was published at which venue?", "o": ["CVPR 2010", "NeurIPS 2012", "ECCV 2014", "ICLR 2016"], "c": 1, "e": "\"ImageNet Classification with Deep Convolutional Neural Networks\" by Krizhevsky, Sutskever & Hinton appeared at NeurIPS 2012.", "tag": null}, {"p": "What does it mean that hand-crafted features have a \"fixed expressive ceiling\"?", "o": ["They can't run on GPUs", "Their representational power is bounded by their human-designed structure", "They have a hard memory limit", "They only support greyscale images"], "c": 1, "e": "No matter how much data you add, SIFT/HOG can only represent what they were engineered to represent. Learned extractors discover richer, task-relevant representations given more data.", "tag": null}]}, {"id": "a2", "ch": "l1213", "g": "l1213-core", "ct": null, "cs": null, "sc": null, "tit": "The MLP Neuron", "b": "L12–13 · §02", "q": [{"p": "What does the net input to a sigmoid neuron compute?", "o": ["net = Σ wᵢxᵢ + b, then σ(net)", "net = Π wᵢxᵢ, then σ(net)", "net = max(wᵢxᵢ), then σ(net)", "net = Σ (wᵢ + xᵢ), then σ(net)"], "c": 0, "e": "A neuron computes a weighted sum plus bias: net = Σwᵢxᵢ + b, then σ(net) = 1/(1+e^{−net}).", "tag": null}, {"p": "What is the derivative of the sigmoid σ(x) = 1/(1 + e⁻ˣ)?", "o": ["σ'(x) = 1 − σ(x)", "σ'(x) = σ(x)·(1 − σ(x))", "σ'(x) = σ(x)²", "σ'(x) = e⁻ˣ"], "c": 1, "e": "σ'(x) = σ(x)·(1−σ(x)). This self-referential derivative makes backprop easy — the forward-pass value is reused.", "tag": null}, {"p": "Why does an MLP neuron need a bias term?", "o": ["To make training faster", "To regularise the weights", "To let the decision boundary translate freely — without it every boundary passes through the origin", "To prevent vanishing gradients"], "c": 2, "e": "Without bias, the hyperplane w·x = 0 always passes through the origin. Bias b gives w·x + b = 0, locatable anywhere in input space.", "tag": null}, {"p": "Where is σ'(x) maximised, and what value does it take?", "o": ["x = 0, value 1", "x = 0, value 0.25", "x = 1, value 0.5", "x → ∞, value 1"], "c": 1, "e": "σ(0) = 0.5, so σ'(0) = 0.25. This maximum of 0.25 is small — one reason sigmoid causes vanishing gradients in deep networks.", "tag": null}, {"p": "Why is the sigmoid problematic for deep networks?", "o": ["Outputs are unbounded", "It's non-differentiable", "At large |x| it saturates, and its peak gradient is only 0.25 — gradients vanish through depth", "It's not invertible"], "c": 2, "e": "In saturated regions σ'(x) ≈ 0. Even at its peak (0.25 < 1), gradients shrink with each layer — exponential vanishing in deep stacks.", "tag": null}, {"p": "What is the range of the sigmoid output?", "o": ["(−∞, ∞)", "[0, 1]", "(0, 1) — open interval", "[−1, 1]"], "c": 2, "e": "σ(x) approaches 0 and 1 but never reaches them. The range is the open interval (0, 1).", "tag": null}, {"p": "What replaced sigmoid as the default activation in modern CNNs?", "o": ["tanh", "ReLU (Rectified Linear Unit)", "Hard sigmoid", "Softplus"], "c": 1, "e": "ReLU(x) = max(0,x) has gradient 1 for positive inputs (no vanishing), is computationally trivial, and empirically converges much faster.", "tag": null}]}, {"id": "a3", "ch": "l1213", "g": "l1213-core", "ct": null, "cs": null, "sc": null, "tit": "Backpropagation", "b": "L12–13 · §03", "q": [{"p": "Why is the MSE loss usually written with a factor of ½: E = ½ Σ (y − o)²?", "o": ["To halve the gradient magnitude", "It's a normalisation constant", "Cosmetic: the ½ cancels when differentiating, leaving a clean (o − y) term", "It enforces a probabilistic interpretation"], "c": 2, "e": "d/do[½(y−o)²] = (o−y). Without the ½ you get 2(o−y). Purely cosmetic.", "tag": null}, {"p": "What are the three factors in ∂E/∂wᵢ at the output layer via the chain rule?", "o": ["(out_o − t)·out_o(1 − out_o)·outₕ", "(out_o − t)·out_o(1 − out_o)·wᵢ", "(out_o − t)²·σ'(net)·outₕ", "(out_o − t)·σ(net)·outₕ"], "c": 0, "e": "∂E/∂wᵢ = (out_o−t)·σ'(net)·outₕ. Since σ'(net) = out_o·(1−out_o), this is the δ-rule: δ_o·outₕ.", "tag": null}, {"p": "What is the weight update rule in gradient descent?", "o": ["wᵢ ← wᵢ + η · ∂E/∂wᵢ", "wᵢ ← wᵢ − η · ∂E/∂wᵢ", "wᵢ ← wᵢ − ∂E/∂wᵢ / η", "wᵢ ← wᵢ · (1 − η · ∂E/∂wᵢ)"], "c": 1, "e": "We subtract η times the gradient. The minus sign moves us in the loss-reducing direction.", "tag": null}, {"p": "For a hidden neuron h feeding output neurons o₁, o₂, …, what is ∂E/∂out_h?", "o": ["Σⱼ ∂E/∂out_{oⱼ} only", "Σⱼ (∂E/∂out_{oⱼ}) · w_{h→oⱼ}", "max_j (∂E/∂out_{oⱼ})", "∂E/∂out_{o₁} only"], "c": 1, "e": "∂E/∂out_h = Σⱼ δ_{oⱼ}·w_{h→oⱼ}. The gradient flows back through every connection weighted by its connection strength.", "tag": null}, {"p": "What is the critical practical detail when computing weight updates in a multi-layer network?", "o": ["Use a different learning rate per layer", "Snapshot all old weights before applying any updates — use the same weights for all gradient computations in a pass", "Always update biases first", "Update the output layer last"], "c": 1, "e": "Updating layer L before computing gradients for L−1 would corrupt those gradients. Snapshot all weights, compute all gradients, then apply all updates.", "tag": null}, {"p": "What's the consequence of η being too large?", "o": ["Painfully slow convergence", "The loss decreases too smoothly", "Divergence or oscillation around the minimum", "Gradients vanish"], "c": 2, "e": "Too large η causes overshooting: the update jumps over the minimum and the loss can increase — oscillation or divergence.", "tag": null}, {"p": "Why is backprop O(forward-pass cost) rather than exponential in depth?", "o": ["Sparse gradients", "GPUs make it fast", "It reuses each layer's ∂E/∂out exactly once — dynamic programming", "Convolutional weight sharing"], "c": 2, "e": "Dynamic programming: backprop caches the error signal at each layer and passes it backward once. No redundant recomputation.", "tag": null}, {"p": "At the output neuron with MSE loss and sigmoid activation, what is ∂E/∂out?", "o": ["out − y", "y − out", "(out − y)²", "σ'(out)"], "c": 0, "e": "E = ½(y−out)². ∂E/∂out = −(y−out) = out−y. Positive when output is too high, negative when too low.", "tag": null}]}, {"id": "a4", "ch": "l1213", "g": "l1213-core", "ct": null, "cs": null, "sc": null, "tit": "Practical Training", "b": "L12–13 · §04", "q": [{"p": "One full pass over all the mini-batches of the training set is called:", "o": ["An iteration", "An epoch", "A batch", "A step"], "c": 1, "e": "Epoch = one complete pass over the entire training set. Iteration = one forward+backward pass on one mini-batch.", "tag": null}, {"p": "Which range covers typical mini-batch sizes for CNN training?", "o": ["1–4", "8–16", "32–256", "1024–4096"], "c": 2, "e": "32–256 is the practical sweet spot. Small batches are noisy; very large batches often generalise worse.", "tag": null}, {"p": "If you double the batch size, what should you typically do to the learning rate η?", "o": ["Halve it", "Leave it unchanged", "Roughly double it (linear scaling rule)", "Set it to zero"], "c": 2, "e": "Linear scaling rule (Goyal et al. 2017): multiply η by the same factor as the batch size, since a larger batch provides a more accurate gradient estimate.", "tag": null}, {"p": "Which dataset is used to tune hyperparameters (η, depth, dropout)?", "o": ["Training set", "Validation set", "Test set", "All three combined"], "c": 1, "e": "Using the test set during training leaks information. All in-training decisions go on the validation set, leaving the test set for one clean final measurement.", "tag": null}, {"p": "What is the cheapest and most reliable regulariser?", "o": ["Heavy weight decay (L2 = 1.0)", "Dropout with p = 0.9", "Early stopping (stop when val loss starts climbing)", "Batch normalisation"], "c": 2, "e": "Early stopping requires no extra computation — just monitor val loss and stop when it starts increasing.", "tag": null}, {"p": "How can you detect overfitting from the training curves?", "o": ["Both train and val losses plateau high", "Train loss → 0 but val loss climbs (or plateaus much higher)", "Train and val losses fall together", "Val loss is always above train loss"], "c": 1, "e": "Overfitting: model memorises training data (train → 0) but fails to generalise (val climbs). The widening gap is the diagnostic signature.", "tag": null}, {"p": "What does the Universal Approximation Theorem (Hornik 1991) state?", "o": ["A deep CNN can fit any continuous function", "A finite MLP with one hidden layer can approximate any continuous function arbitrarily well", "Sigmoid neurons are universal Turing machines", "Cross-entropy is the optimal loss for any task"], "c": 1, "e": "UAT: a single-hidden-layer MLP with enough neurons and a non-polynomial activation can approximate any continuous function on a compact set.", "tag": null}, {"p": "Despite UAT, why are deep networks strongly preferred over wide-shallow MLPs?", "o": ["Wide-shallow MLPs cannot represent classification boundaries", "Depth is needed for parameter efficiency and trainability, not for representability", "UAT was disproved", "Deep nets have no vanishing-gradient problem"], "c": 1, "e": "UAT guarantees representability but may require exponential width. Deep networks represent the same functions with far fewer parameters by composing simple transformations.", "tag": null}]}, {"id": "a5", "ch": "l1213", "g": "l1213-core", "ct": null, "cs": null, "sc": null, "tit": "From MLP to CNN", "b": "L12–13 · §05", "q": [{"p": "What is the main spatial problem with feeding images into an MLP?", "o": ["MLPs cannot handle colour channels", "MLPs flatten the image and lose all spatial structure — adjacent pixels are no closer in the representation than opposite corners", "MLPs require square images", "MLPs use too much memory"], "c": 1, "e": "Flattening 224×224×3 to 150,528-d discards all spatial relationships. CNNs maintain spatial structure throughout.", "tag": null}, {"p": "Which two natural-image properties do CNNs exploit by design?", "o": ["Compressibility and sparsity", "Locality and translation invariance", "Symmetry and orthogonality", "Differentiability and continuity"], "c": 1, "e": "Locality: meaningful patterns are local. Translation invariance: the same pattern is useful wherever it appears. CNNs encode both via shared local kernels.", "tag": null}, {"p": "Convolutional weight-sharing enforces which property?", "o": ["Scale invariance", "Rotation invariance", "Translation invariance", "Colour invariance"], "c": 2, "e": "The same K×K filter at every spatial position means the same detector fires wherever the pattern appears — translation invariance.", "tag": null}, {"p": "Compared to an equivalent fully-connected layer, a conv layer has:", "o": ["More parameters", "Roughly the same parameters", "Vastly fewer parameters — the K×K kernel is reused across all spatial positions", "Zero parameters"], "c": 2, "e": "An FC connecting 224×224 input to output needs 224⁴ ≈ 2.5B weights. A 3×3 conv needs just 9 (reused at every position).", "tag": null}, {"p": "\"The same K×K kernel is applied at every spatial position.\" This is called:", "o": ["Parameter scaling", "Pixel pooling", "Weight sharing", "Dilation"], "c": 2, "e": "Weight sharing: one set of K×K weights serves the entire spatial domain.", "tag": null}, {"p": "What does it mean for a feature to be detected with translation invariance?", "o": ["It can only be detected at fixed positions", "The same parameters detect it regardless of where it appears in the image", "It requires data augmentation", "It doesn't move during training"], "c": 1, "e": "Translation invariance: a detector fires wherever the pattern appears — top-left, centre, bottom-right — using the same learned weights.", "tag": null}]}, {"id": "a6", "ch": "l1213", "g": "l1213-core", "ct": null, "cs": null, "sc": null, "tit": "CNN Layers in Detail", "b": "L12–13 · §06", "q": [{"p": "The output spatial size of a conv layer is given by:", "o": ["⌊(W + K − 2P) / S⌋ + 1", "⌊(W − K + 2P) / S⌋ + 1", "⌊(W · K + P) / S⌋", "W − K · S + P"], "c": 1, "e": "Output = ⌊(W − K + 2P)/S⌋ + 1.", "tag": null}, {"p": "Given input 28×28, kernel 5×5, padding P = 0, stride S = 1, the output spatial size is:", "o": ["28", "24", "23", "26"], "c": 1, "e": "⌊(28−5)/1⌋+1 = 24.", "tag": null}, {"p": "A 3×3 conv with 64 input channels and 64 output channels has how many learned weights (ignoring biases)?", "o": ["192", "4,096", "36,864", "147,456"], "c": 2, "e": "3 × 3 × 64 × 64 = 36,864.", "tag": null}, {"p": "How many learned weights does a max-pooling layer have?", "o": ["1 per channel", "K² per channel", "K² · Cin", "Zero — it's a fixed operation"], "c": 3, "e": "Max pooling selects the maximum in each K×K window. No learnable parameters.", "tag": null}, {"p": "Typical max-pool window and stride in classical CNNs:", "o": ["1×1, stride 1", "2×2, stride 2", "4×4, stride 4", "7×7, stride 1"], "c": 1, "e": "2×2 stride 2 halves spatial resolution with minimal information loss.", "tag": null}, {"p": "Which of the following is NOT a reason ReLU replaced sigmoid in deep CNNs?", "o": ["Breaks associativity of stacked linear ops, adding non-linearity", "Gradient is 1 on positives — no vanishing through depth", "Computationally cheap — single comparison, no exponential", "Produces outputs in (0,1) suitable for probabilities"], "c": 3, "e": "ReLU outputs [0, ∞), not (0,1). Outputs in (0,1) is a property of sigmoid. The other three are genuine reasons to prefer ReLU.", "tag": null}, {"p": "What has Local Response Normalisation (LRN, from AlexNet) been replaced by in modern designs?", "o": ["Dropout", "Batch Normalization (Ioffe & Szegedy 2015)", "Weight decay", "Skip connections"], "c": 1, "e": "LRN was superseded by Batch Norm, which is more principled and effective at preventing internal covariate shift.", "tag": null}, {"p": "The triple \"conv → ReLU → pool\" is loosely called:", "o": ["A residual block", "A convolutional layer", "An inception module", "A skip connection"], "c": 1, "e": "In common usage, \"convolutional layer\" refers to the full conv→ReLU→pool block, treated as a single logical unit.", "tag": null}]}, {"id": "a7", "ch": "l1213", "g": "l1213-core", "ct": null, "cs": null, "sc": null, "tit": "AlexNet (2012)", "b": "L12–13 · §07", "q": [{"p": "AlexNet has how many conv layers and how many FC layers?", "o": ["3 conv + 2 FC", "5 conv + 3 FC", "8 conv + 1 FC", "11 conv + 4 FC"], "c": 1, "e": "5 conv layers + 3 FC layers (4096, 4096, 1000-way softmax). 8 learnable layers total.", "tag": null}, {"p": "Approximately how many total parameters does AlexNet have?", "o": ["~5 million", "~25 million", "~60 million", "~138 million"], "c": 2, "e": "~60M parameters. VGG-16 for comparison has ~138M.", "tag": null}, {"p": "What fraction of AlexNet's parameters live in the FC layers?", "o": ["~10%", "~50%", "~75%", "~95% (≈ 58M of 60M)"], "c": 3, "e": "The three FC layers account for ~58M of ~60M parameters. The 5 conv layers use only ~3M.", "tag": null}, {"p": "AlexNet's Conv1 uses what kernel size and stride?", "o": ["3×3, stride 1", "5×5, stride 2", "11×11, stride 4", "7×7, stride 2"], "c": 2, "e": "11×11 kernel with stride 4 reduces 224×224 to 55×55 in one step. Aggressive downsampling motivated by compute cost.", "tag": null}, {"p": "Which technique did AlexNet NOT introduce or popularise?", "o": ["ReLU in place of sigmoid for hidden activations", "Dropout in FC layers", "Local Response Normalisation", "Residual / skip connections"], "c": 3, "e": "Residual skip connections were introduced by ResNet (He et al., 2015). AlexNet popularised ReLU, dropout, and LRN.", "tag": null}, {"p": "Why did AlexNet rely heavily on data augmentation (random crops, flips, PCA colour jitter)?", "o": ["It added new training data", "To prevent the ~58M FC parameters from memorising the training set", "It's required by the ImageNet rules", "To speed up training"], "c": 1, "e": "With ~58M parameters and 1.2M images, overfitting was a real risk. Augmentation multiplies coverage without collecting new labelled images.", "tag": null}, {"p": "AlexNet was trained on how many GPUs, and for roughly how long?", "o": ["1 GPU, 1 day", "2 GPUs (3 GB each), about 6 days", "8 GPUs, 1 hour", "16 GPUs, 1 month"], "c": 1, "e": "Two NVIDIA GTX 580 GPUs (3 GB VRAM each) for roughly 5–6 days.", "tag": null}]}, {"id": "a8", "ch": "l1213", "g": "l1213-core", "ct": null, "cs": null, "sc": null, "tit": "LeNet-5 (1998)", "b": "L12–13 · §08", "q": [{"p": "LeNet-5 was used commercially for which task?", "o": ["Face recognition", "Cheque reading (digit OCR)", "Medical imaging", "Industrial inspection"], "c": 1, "e": "LeCun et al.'s LeNet-5 was deployed by banks for reading handwritten digits on cheques — the first commercially successful CNN, 14 years before AlexNet.", "tag": null}, {"p": "What activation function did LeNet use?", "o": ["ReLU", "Tanh / sigmoid", "Leaky ReLU", "GELU"], "c": 1, "e": "Pre-ReLU era. AlexNet's switch to ReLU was a major contributor to faster training.", "tag": null}, {"p": "How many convolutional layers does LeNet-5 have?", "o": ["1", "2", "5", "8"], "c": 1, "e": "Two conv layers (C1 and C3) interleaved with two pooling layers. The \"5\" refers to total learnable layers.", "tag": null}, {"p": "Given 28×28×1 MNIST input through Conv1 (20 filters, 5×5, stride 1, no padding), the output is:", "o": ["28×28×20", "24×24×20", "23×23×20", "14×14×20"], "c": 1, "e": "⌊(28−5)/1⌋+1 = 24. With 20 filters: 24×24×20.", "tag": null}, {"p": "Which of these features did LeNet lack that AlexNet introduced?", "o": ["Convolution", "Pooling", "ReLU, dropout, and any form of normalisation", "Backpropagation"], "c": 2, "e": "LeNet had convolution, pooling and backpropagation. AlexNet added ReLU, dropout and LRN — all critical for scaling to ImageNet.", "tag": null}, {"p": "LeNet's pooling is typically:", "o": ["3×3 stride 2", "2×2 stride 2", "5×5 stride 1", "Global average pooling"], "c": 1, "e": "2×2 stride 2 subsampling — the simplest possible spatial halving.", "tag": null}]}, {"id": "n1", "ch": "l1213", "g": "l1213-applied", "ct": null, "cs": null, "sc": null, "tit": "L12–13 Applied Questions", "b": "L12–13 · Applied", "q": [{"p": "For an output neuron o connected to hidden neuron h (sigmoid activation, MSE loss L = ½(o − t)²), which expression correctly gives ∂L/∂w via the chain rule?", "o": ["(out_o − t) · out_o · outₕ", "(out_o − t) · out_o(1 − out_o) · outₕ", "(out_o − t)² · σ'(net) · outₕ", "(out_o − t) · out_o(1 − out_o) · w"], "c": 1, "e": "∂L/∂w = (∂L/∂out_o)·(∂out_o/∂net)·(∂net/∂w) = (out_o−t)·σ'(net)·outₕ. Since σ'(net) = out_o·(1−out_o), this is the classic δ-rule: δ_o·outₕ.", "tag": null}, {"p": "A conv layer takes a 256×256×3 image with 32 filters of size 5×5, padding P = 1, stride S = 2. What is the output shape?", "o": ["128×128×32", "127×127×32", "126×126×32", "254×254×32"], "c": 1, "e": "Spatial dim = ⌊(256 + 2·1 − 5)/2⌋ + 1 = ⌊253/2⌋ + 1 = 127. With 32 filters: 127×127×32.", "tag": "Shape"}, {"p": "Two stacked conv layers with no non-linearity between them are equivalent to a single conv layer. What is the implication for deep CNN design?", "o": ["Depth adds no expressive power without non-linearities; activations such as ReLU between layers are essential to break this associativity", "Deeper networks always perform better regardless of activation functions", "This makes deeper networks faster to train", "The convolution becomes separable, which is more expressive"], "c": 0, "e": "Convolution is associative: (I*L)*H = I*(L*H). Without a non-linearity, depth collapses to one equivalent convolution. ReLU breaks this via max(0,x). Leaky ReLU (gain ≈ 0.2) is a common alternative that avoids dying neurons.", "tag": null}, {"p": "Why is a separate validation set used in addition to training and test sets?", "o": ["To provide extra training data when the training set is small", "For early stopping, hyperparameter tuning, and architecture selection — the test set is reserved for final unbiased evaluation only", "The validation set is used to compute the final published accuracy", "It replaces the test set when the dataset is large"], "c": 1, "e": "Using the test set during training leaks information and produces optimistically biased estimates. The validation set absorbs all in-training decisions, leaving the test set for one clean final measurement.", "tag": null}, {"p": "Which two augmentation operations did AlexNet use, and what is the primary reason augmentation reduces overfitting?", "o": ["Rotation and scale jitter — improves rotation invariance", "Random 224×224 crops from 256×256 images and horizontal reflections — multiplies effective dataset coverage and makes memorisation of specific images harder", "Gaussian blur and sharpening — reduces high-frequency noise sensitivity", "Colour normalisation and contrast stretching — handles illumination variation"], "c": 1, "e": "AlexNet: (i) random 224×224 crops from 256×256 padded images; (ii) horizontal reflections. With ~60M parameters and 1.2M images, augmentation ensures each epoch sees different variants without collecting new labels.", "tag": null}]}, {"id": "a9", "ch": "l1416", "g": "l1416-core", "ct": "Lectures 14–16", "cs": "CNN Architectures, Retrieval & Interpretability", "sc": null, "tit": "VGG & the Case for Depth", "b": "L14–16 · §09", "q": [{"p": "What is VGG's key uniform design principle?", "o": ["Layers vary widely: 11×11, 7×7, 3×3 mixed", "Every conv is 3×3, every pool is 2×2; channels double when spatial halves", "Use 1×1 convs throughout", "Adaptive kernel sizes per layer"], "c": 1, "e": "VGG replaced AlexNet's bespoke per-layer design with one uniform recipe: 3×3 conv with ReLU, 2×2 max pool. Depth was the only variable.", "tag": null}, {"p": "Why do two stacked 3×3 convs beat one 5×5 conv?", "o": ["Same receptive field, but fewer parameters (2·9 vs 25 per channel) AND an extra non-linearity", "Larger receptive field for the same cost", "Only the 3×3 stack can use padding", "5×5 isn't differentiable"], "c": 0, "e": "Two 3×3 convs cover the same 5×5 receptive field but use 18 vs 25 weights, plus an extra ReLU.", "tag": null}, {"p": "Three stacked 3×3 convs are equivalent in receptive field to:", "o": ["one 5×5", "one 7×7", "one 9×9", "one 11×11"], "c": 1, "e": "Each 3×3 adds 2 to the receptive field. Three stacked: 3+2+2 = 7. Three 3×3 use 27 vs 49 weights for one 7×7.", "tag": null}, {"p": "Approximately how many parameters does VGG-16's FC6 layer alone have?", "o": ["~4M", "~38M", "~103M", "~138M"], "c": 2, "e": "FC6: 7×7×512 = 25,088 inputs → 4,096 outputs: 25,088 × 4,096 ≈ 102.8M weights.", "tag": null}, {"p": "VGG-16's approximate total parameter count:", "o": ["~60M", "~100M", "~138M", "~250M"], "c": 2, "e": "~138M parameters. The three FC layers account for ~124M; the 13 conv layers use only ~14M.", "tag": null}, {"p": "VGG-16's top-5 error on ImageNet:", "o": ["18.9%", "13.5%", "7.3%", "3.6%"], "c": 2, "e": "VGG-16 achieved ~7.3% top-5 error — cutting AlexNet's 18.9% by more than half through depth alone.", "tag": null}, {"p": "Which model uses mixed kernel sizes (11×11, 5×5, 3×3) at different layers?", "o": ["LeNet-5", "AlexNet", "VGG-16", "ResNet"], "c": 1, "e": "AlexNet used 11×11 at Conv1, 5×5 at Conv2, and 3×3 at Conv3–5. VGG abandoned bespoke design for uniform 3×3.", "tag": null}]}, {"id": "a10", "ch": "l1416", "g": "l1416-core", "ct": null, "cs": null, "sc": null, "tit": "GoogLeNet / Inception", "b": "L14–16 · §10", "q": [{"p": "Which two problems with VGG did the Inception architecture address?", "o": ["Slow inference and high latency", "Single-scale conv layers, and FC layers containing ~90% of parameters", "Vanishing gradients and exploding gradients", "Lack of skip connections"], "c": 1, "e": "VGG's layers see only one scale per layer and its FC layers are parameter behemoths. Inception fixes both: multi-scale parallel convolutions plus FC-replacing global average pooling.", "tag": null}, {"p": "An Inception module runs which branches in parallel?", "o": ["1×1, 3×3, 5×5 convolutions plus a pooling branch — outputs concatenated", "A conv layer and a fully-connected layer", "Forward pass and backward pass simultaneously", "Three identical 3×3 convs"], "c": 0, "e": "Multiple receptive-field sizes in one layer, plus a pool branch. All four outputs concatenated channel-wise. 1×1 convs serve as cheap bottlenecks.", "tag": null}, {"p": "GoogLeNet replaces the parameter-heavy FC layers with:", "o": ["Larger FC layers with dropout", "Global Average Pooling — collapse each H×W feature map to a single number by averaging", "Max pooling everywhere", "Recurrent layers"], "c": 1, "e": "GAP: 7×7×1024 → 1×1×1024 with zero learnable parameters, then one small FC. This reduced GoogLeNet to ~7M parameters vs VGG's ~138M.", "tag": null}, {"p": "Global Average Pooling adds how many learnable parameters?", "o": ["1 per channel", "K² per channel", "Zero — it's parameter-free", "Same as a 1×1 conv"], "c": 2, "e": "GAP averages H×W values to one number per channel. No weights, no biases.", "tag": null}, {"p": "What are auxiliary classifiers in GoogLeNet, and why are they used?", "o": ["Backup classifiers used at inference if the main one fails", "Intermediate softmax heads at middle layers, each with their own loss, used during training to combat vanishing gradients (removed at inference)", "Ensemble teachers for distillation", "Cross-validation classifiers"], "c": 1, "e": "GoogLeNet's 22 layers made gradients struggle to reach early layers. Auxiliary classifiers inject extra gradient signal at intermediate depths during training and are removed at inference.", "tag": null}, {"p": "GoogLeNet has approximately how many parameters compared to VGG-16?", "o": ["~7M vs ~138M — roughly 20× fewer", "About the same", "More than VGG", "~50M vs ~138M"], "c": 0, "e": "GoogLeNet ~7M, VGG-16 ~138M. The ~20× reduction comes from global average pooling and 1×1 bottlenecks.", "tag": null}, {"p": "The four parallel branches in an Inception module are combined by:", "o": ["Element-wise addition", "Element-wise multiplication", "Channel-wise concatenation", "Average pooling across branches"], "c": 2, "e": "All four outputs at the same spatial resolution are concatenated along the channel dimension.", "tag": null}]}, {"id": "a11", "ch": "l1416", "g": "l1416-core", "ct": null, "cs": null, "sc": null, "tit": "ResNet", "b": "L14–16 · §11", "q": [{"p": "What is the \"degradation problem\" that motivated ResNet?", "o": ["Gradients explode in deep networks", "Models overfit when too deep", "Plain nets deeper than ~20 layers got worse on training loss too — not just vanishing gradients or overfitting", "GPUs ran out of memory"], "c": 2, "e": "He et al. showed a 56-layer plain net had higher training error than a 20-layer one. Degradation is fundamentally different from overfitting.", "tag": null}, {"p": "A ResNet residual block computes:", "o": ["output = F(x)", "output = F(x) + x (identity skip)", "output = F(x) · x", "output = F(x) ⊕ x (channel concat)"], "c": 1, "e": "H(x) = F(x) + x. The skip connection adds the input to the transformed output. The layers learn the residual F(x) = H(x) − x.", "tag": null}, {"p": "Layers inside a residual block effectively learn what kind of mapping?", "o": ["The full input-to-output transformation", "Small residual corrections to the identity", "Random Gaussian noise", "Inverse transformations"], "c": 1, "e": "Because the skip already provides the identity, the layers only need to learn the deviation — small corrections are easier to learn than full transformations.", "tag": null}, {"p": "Why is identity a good resting state for a layer?", "o": ["It minimises memory", "If F learns zero, the block is the identity — strictly no worse than removing it; gradient also flows cleanly through the skip", "Backprop requires it", "It enforces sparsity"], "c": 1, "e": "If a layer is not needed, it learns F→0, passing x unchanged. Adding layers can never hurt in theory. The skip also provides a gradient highway.", "tag": null}, {"p": "The deepest ResNet variant from the original He et al. paper:", "o": ["18 layers", "50 layers", "152 layers", "1001 layers"], "c": 2, "e": "He et al. (CVPR 2016) tested ResNet-18, -34, -50, -101 and -152. 1001-layer variants appeared in a follow-up, not the original.", "tag": null}, {"p": "ResNet's top-5 error on ImageNet (original paper):", "o": ["7.3%", "6.7%", "3.57%", "1.2%"], "c": 2, "e": "ResNet-152 ensemble: 3.57% top-5 error — surpassing human-level performance (~5%) for the first time.", "tag": null}]}, {"id": "a12", "ch": "l1416", "g": "l1416-core", "ct": null, "cs": null, "sc": null, "tit": "Training Mechanics", "b": "L14–16 · §12", "q": [{"p": "Which loss is standard for multi-class classification CNNs?", "o": ["Mean squared error", "Cross-entropy / log-loss", "Hinge loss", "Triplet loss"], "c": 1, "e": "Cross-entropy E = −log(p_correct) penalises confident wrong predictions heavily and pairs naturally with softmax.", "tag": null}, {"p": "Cross-entropy E = −log(p_correct) heavily penalises:", "o": ["Underconfident correct predictions", "Confident wrong predictions", "Predictions equal to 0.5", "Predictions equal to 1.0"], "c": 1, "e": "If the correct class has probability 0.01, −log(0.01) = 4.6. If 0.99, −log(0.99) ≈ 0.01. Loss explodes as the model becomes confidently wrong.", "tag": null}, {"p": "ReLU's gradient during backprop:", "o": ["Always 1", "Always 0", "Equal to 1 where input was positive, 0 elsewhere", "Equal to σ(x)·(1 − σ(x))"], "c": 2, "e": "ReLU(x) = x if x > 0, 0 otherwise. Derivative: 1 if x > 0 (passes through), 0 if x ≤ 0. Far better for depth than sigmoid's ≤ 0.25.", "tag": null}, {"p": "During backprop through max-pool, the gradient flows to:", "o": ["All positions in the pooling window equally", "The arg-max position only; other positions get zero", "The centre of the window", "The smallest input"], "c": 1, "e": "Max-pool is differentiable almost everywhere. Gradient = 1 at the arg-max position, 0 elsewhere.", "tag": null}, {"p": "Gradient with respect to a conv weight is computed how?", "o": ["Sum over all spatial positions where the weight was applied, of (upstream gradient × corresponding input value)", "Single dot-product, like an FC layer", "Random sampling of positions", "Average across channels"], "c": 0, "e": "Weight sharing means each weight was applied at every spatial position. Its gradient accumulates contributions from all those positions.", "tag": null}, {"p": "Gradient with respect to a conv layer's input is computed by:", "o": ["Forward conv with the kernel", "Transposed convolution of the upstream gradient with the (rotated) kernel — the same op that becomes \"up-conv\" in FCNs", "Element-wise division", "Matrix inverse of the kernel"], "c": 1, "e": "To backpropagate through a conv layer, you convolve the upstream gradient with a 180°-rotated kernel — a transposed convolution, identical to up-conv in FCN/U-Net decoders.", "tag": null}, {"p": "AlexNet's ~60M weights — how many dimensions does the gradient vector have?", "o": ["1000", "~60 million (one per weight)", "Just the FC weights", "Depends on batch size"], "c": 1, "e": "Gradient descent operates in the full weight space. With ~60M parameters, the gradient is a ~60M-dimensional vector.", "tag": null}]}, {"id": "a13", "ch": "l1416", "g": "l1416-core", "ct": null, "cs": null, "sc": null, "tit": "Initialisation & Fine-Tuning", "b": "L14–16 · §13", "q": [{"p": "Why can't you initialise all weights in an MLP/CNN to zero?", "o": ["Division by zero in backprop", "All neurons in a layer compute the same output and receive the same gradient — symmetry is never broken", "Gradient descent diverges", "Bias terms become undefined"], "c": 1, "e": "With all weights zero, every neuron in a layer computes the same thing. Their gradients are identical — the network can never learn different features.", "tag": null}, {"p": "For shallow nets (~8 layers), what Gaussian init usually works?", "o": ["σ ≈ 0.01, mean 0", "σ ≈ 100, mean 1", "All zeros", "σ ≈ √(input_count)"], "c": 0, "e": "Small Gaussian (σ ≈ 0.01, mean 0) works well enough for shallow nets. Deeper nets need Xavier or He init.", "tag": null}, {"p": "He initialisation (for ReLU networks) scales σ by:", "o": ["σ = 1", "σ = √(2 / n_in)", "σ = 1 / (n_in + n_out)", "σ = 1 / √(epochs)"], "c": 1, "e": "He et al. (2015): σ = √(2/n_in). The factor of 2 accounts for ReLU zeroing half of inputs on average.", "tag": null}, {"p": "Which three observations motivate fine-tuning instead of training from scratch?", "o": ["Low compute, simple loss, sparse data", "Early conv1 filters across CNNs look nearly identical (Gabor-like); deep nets need lots of data; labels are expensive", "GPUs run hot; SSDs are slow; networks overfit", "Backprop is non-local; weights are unstable; lr is hard to choose"], "c": 1, "e": "(1) Low-level features are reusable across image tasks. (2) Learning from scratch needs millions of labelled images. (3) Labelling is expensive.", "tag": null}, {"p": "In Caffe, freezing the early conv layers during fine-tuning is done by setting:", "o": ["lr_mult: 0 on those layers", "requires_grad: false", "train: false", "frozen: true"], "c": 0, "e": "lr_mult: 0 sets the learning rate multiplier to zero — parameters receive gradients but the update step is scaled to zero.", "tag": null}, {"p": "After freezing early layers in fine-tuning, what do you typically replace?", "o": ["The convolutional backbone", "The final FC / classifier layer with a fresh randomly-initialised one of the right output size", "The activation functions", "The pooling layers"], "c": 1, "e": "The final layer maps features to class scores. For a new task with different classes, this layer must be replaced and trained from scratch.", "tag": null}, {"p": "Which is NOT a common reason to use fine-tuning in practice?", "o": ["You have a small labelled dataset", "Training from scratch takes weeks of GPU time", "You want to benefit from generic low-level features learned on large datasets", "Your task is fundamentally different from anything pre-training has seen"], "c": 3, "e": "If the target task is truly dissimilar (e.g., ImageNet model on medical infrared), pre-trained features may not transfer and training from scratch could be better.", "tag": null}]}, {"id": "n2", "ch": "l1416", "g": "l14-applied", "ct": null, "cs": null, "sc": "Lecture 14 · Applied Questions", "tit": "L14 Applied Questions", "b": "L14 · Applied", "q": [{"p": "Which statement correctly pairs each architecture with its defining design idea?", "o": ["AlexNet — stacks of 3×3; VGG — first GPU deep net; GoogLeNet — residual skip connections", "AlexNet — first deep CNN combining ReLU/dropout/GPU training to win ImageNet by ~10%; VGG — depth via stacks of 3×3 convs; GoogLeNet — Inception modules with parallel scales, 1×1 bottlenecks, auxiliary losses", "AlexNet — Inception modules; VGG — auxiliary losses; GoogLeNet — 11×11 kernels", "All three use the same design; they differ only in depth"], "c": 1, "e": "AlexNet (2012): first to combine ReLU, dropout, LRN and GPU training to win ImageNet by ~10%. VGG (2014): uniform 3×3 stacks — depth alone provides capacity. GoogLeNet (2015): Inception modules run multiple scales in parallel, 1×1 bottlenecks, auxiliary classifiers.", "tag": null}, {"p": "When fine-tuning AlexNet for a 3-class medical imaging task, what is the standard recipe?", "o": ["Re-initialise all weights and train at the full ImageNet learning rate", "Replace the final 1000-way FC with a fresh 3-way layer; freeze early conv layers (lr_mult: 0); train remaining at ~10× lower learning rate; heavy augmentation; early stopping", "Add three extra FC layers on top without modification", "Copy weights unchanged and add only a softmax re-scaling layer"], "c": 1, "e": "Recipe: (1) Replace final FC with a 3-way randomly-initialised layer; (2) Freeze conv1–3; (3) Train conv4–5 and new FC head at low lr; (4) Heavy augmentation for the small dataset; (5) Early-stop on val loss divergence.", "tag": null}, {"p": "What problem does Xavier (Glorot) initialisation solve, and what formula does it apply?", "o": ["Prevents overfitting by reducing all weights toward zero", "Addresses exploding/vanishing activation magnitudes through deep layers by scaling σ ∝ 1/√(n_in + n_out) so activation magnitudes stay roughly constant across layers", "Initialises biases to 1 to ensure positive initial activations", "Sets all weights in a layer to the same value"], "c": 1, "e": "A fixed-σ Gaussian amplifies or shrinks activations exponentially through depth. Xavier scales σ so each layer's output variance matches its input: σ = 1/(n_in + n_out) or √(2/n). For ReLU, He init doubles this to √(2/n_in).", "tag": null}]}, {"id": "n3", "ch": "l1416", "g": "l15-retrieval", "ct": null, "cs": null, "sc": "Lecture 15 · Deep Image Retrieval (SBIR)", "tit": "Deep Image Retrieval", "b": "L15 · Retrieval", "q": [{"p": "In the triplet loss L(a,p,n) = max(0, ‖f(a)−f(p)‖² − ‖f(a)−f(n)‖² + m), when is the gradient zero?", "o": ["When the anchor and positive embeddings are identical", "When ‖f(a)−f(n)‖² ≥ ‖f(a)−f(p)‖² + m — the negative is already at least margin m further from the anchor than the positive", "When margin m equals zero", "Whenever the loss value exceeds 1.0"], "c": 1, "e": "When the negative is already far enough away, the inner expression is ≤ 0 and max clips it to 0. Zero loss → zero gradient — an \"easy\" triplet contributing nothing to learning. Mining hard/semi-hard negatives is crucial.", "tag": null}, {"p": "Why is a triplet-retrieval network commonly pre-trained with softmax loss before switching to triplet loss?", "o": ["Softmax pre-training is always faster and needs fewer GPUs", "From random weights, most triplets are easy negatives (zero gradient). Softmax pre-training structures the embedding space so similar classes cluster, giving triplet training many informative hard triplets from the start", "Softmax and triplet losses are equivalent; pre-training just warms up the GPU", "Triplet loss cannot be computed without an existing softmax classifier layer"], "c": 1, "e": "With random initialisation, the embedding space is unstructured — most random negatives are already far away (easy), so gradients are sparse. Softmax pre-training imposes class structure, ensuring triplet training begins with plentiful hard negatives.", "tag": null}, {"p": "Why can't Average Precision be optimised directly as a training loss, and what is the standard fix?", "o": ["AP is computationally too expensive to calculate for large databases", "AP contains a step-function indicator for item relevance that is non-differentiable; the standard fix is a soft differentiable surrogate replacing the indicator with soft binning across quantised similarity intervals", "AP only applies to binary classification and cannot be extended to retrieval", "AP values are bounded [0,1], making gradients too small for effective training"], "c": 1, "e": "AP = (1/R) Σₖ P(k)·rel(k) where rel(k) is a step indicator. Step functions have zero gradient almost everywhere. AP Loss (He et al. CVPR 2018) and Smooth-AP replace rel(k) with a sigmoid-based soft approximation, giving a differentiable proxy.", "tag": null}]}, {"id": "c1", "ch": "l1416", "g": "l16-cnn", "ct": null, "cs": null, "sc": "Lecture 16 · CNN Interpretability", "tit": "Evaluating Classifier Performance", "b": "L16 · §01", "q": [{"p": "A test set contains 500 cat images and 10 dog images. A classifier gets every cat right and every dog wrong. Without row-wise normalisation, what would the raw mAP be, and why is this misleading?", "o": ["~98% — misleading because it ignores the dog class entirely", "50% — it averages 100% cat accuracy and 0% dog accuracy equally", "100% — the majority class dominates and masks the failure on dogs", "0% — any misclassification on a class sets the whole score to zero"], "c": 0, "e": "~98% is correct. Without normalisation, the 500 correct cat predictions vastly outnumber the 10 wrong dog predictions. The figure looks almost perfect while completely hiding that the classifier fails entirely on one class.", "tag": null}, {"p": "In a normalised 4-class confusion matrix, the diagonal values are 1.0, 0.5, 0.5, 1.0. What is the mAP?", "o": ["0.625", "0.75", "0.50", "1.0"], "c": 1, "e": "mAP = mean of diagonal = (1.0 + 0.5 + 0.5 + 1.0)/4 = 0.75.", "tag": null}, {"p": "In a confusion matrix, rows represent ground truth and columns represent predictions. A value of 0.3 in row \"Cat\", column \"Dog\" means what?", "o": ["30% of images predicted as Dog were actually Cats", "30% of actual Cat images were incorrectly classified as Dog", "The model is 30% confident that Cats are Dogs", "30 Cat images were in the test set"], "c": 1, "e": "Rows are ground truth, columns are predictions. Row \"Cat\", column \"Dog\" = of all images that ARE cats, 30% were predicted as dogs.", "tag": null}, {"p": "AlexNet achieves a top-5 error rate of 18.9% on ImageNet. What does this mean precisely?", "o": ["18.9% of images were classified incorrectly as the top-1 prediction", "For 18.9% of test images, the correct class did not appear in the five highest-scoring softmax outputs", "The model's top-5 predictions are wrong 18.9% more often than top-1", "18.9% of the 1000 ImageNet classes were never correctly predicted"], "c": 1, "e": "Top-5 error = fraction of test images where none of the five highest-scoring outputs matched the ground truth. 18.9% error means 81.1% top-5 accuracy.", "tag": null}, {"p": "AlexNet's softmax output: motor scooter 0.41, golf cart 0.22, go-kart 0.18, moped 0.11, bumper car 0.05, bicycle 0.03. Ground truth: motor scooter. What are the top-1 and top-5 accuracies?", "o": ["Top-1: 1, Top-5: 1", "Top-1: 0, Top-5: 1", "Top-1: 1, Top-5: 0", "Top-1: 0.41, Top-5: 0.97"], "c": 0, "e": "Motor scooter is the top-1 prediction and the ground truth → top-1 = 1. It also appears in the top 5 → top-5 = 1.", "tag": null}, {"p": "Confusion matrix diagonal [0.9, 0.9, 0.3] for 3 classes. When is this more informative than a single overall accuracy figure?", "o": ["When the test set is perfectly balanced", "When class C is rare — overall accuracy would hide the poor C performance", "When you only care about the majority class", "When the model uses softmax"], "c": 1, "e": "When class C is rare, raw accuracy is dominated by common classes. A model scoring 90%, 90%, 30% would show ~90% overall — hiding the failure on C.", "tag": null}, {"p": "Why does the diagonal of a normalised confusion matrix correspond to per-class AP, and why does its mean equal mAP?", "o": ["The diagonal values are probabilities that sum to 1.0", "Each diagonal entry = proportion of that class correctly identified; averaging these gives mean top-1 precision across all classes", "The diagonal measures recall, not precision", "It only applies when all classes have equal test-set sizes"], "c": 1, "e": "After row-wise normalisation, diagonal[i] = correct_i / total_i = per-class AP (top-1 sense). Mean = mAP.", "tag": null}]}, {"id": "c2", "ch": "l1416", "g": "l16-cnn", "ct": null, "cs": null, "sc": null, "tit": "Activation Maximisation", "b": "L16 · §02", "q": [{"p": "In Activation Maximisation, what is updated and what is frozen during backpropagation?", "o": ["The image is frozen; only the network weights are updated", "Both image pixels and weights are updated simultaneously", "The network weights are frozen; only the image pixels are updated", "Only the final FC layer weights are updated"], "c": 2, "e": "This is the core inversion. In training: image fixed, update weights. In AM: network frozen, update only pixel values.", "tag": null}, {"p": "The AM loss is L(x) = ‖f(x) − y_target‖² + λ·TV(x). What does λ control at the two extremes?", "o": ["λ controls learning rate — too high causes divergence", "λ controls smoothness — too high gives indistinct blur, too low gives high-frequency noise", "λ controls how many classes are suppressed", "λ controls the number of iterations"], "c": 1, "e": "Very large λ forces extreme smoothness — featureless blob. Very small λ allows high-frequency noise — unintelligible static.", "tag": null}, {"p": "TV regularisation penalises high-frequency noise. What is the intuitive connection between TV and edge detection?", "o": ["Both use pooling to reduce spatial resolution", "TV = sum of pixel differences between neighbours — the same operation as an edge detector; many edges = high TV, which is penalised", "TV uses convolution kernels identical to Sobel filters", "Both use Gaussian blur as pre-processing"], "c": 1, "e": "TV = Σ|x_{i,j}−x_{i+1,j}| + |x_{i,j}−x_{i,j+1}|. Precisely what an edge detector measures. High TV = many edges = noisy image.", "tag": null}, {"p": "You run AM on the \"dumbbell\" class four independent times. Each run shows a different image but every image contains an arm. What does this reveal?", "o": ["AM is not working correctly", "Arms are a spurious feature; the network ignores them", "The network has learned arms as a necessary feature of \"dumbbell\" due to training data bias; different runs find different local minima but all require arms", "The TV regulariser is introducing arm-shaped artefacts"], "c": 2, "e": "Different random initialisations find different local minima, but the structural requirement (arms) is consistent — revealing the network genuinely learned this bias from training data.", "tag": null}, {"p": "Why is AM a better diagnostic tool than searching training images for the highest-scoring class examples?", "o": ["AM is faster to compute", "Training image search reveals data correlation; AM reveals causation — what the network genuinely needs to fire, independently of dataset composition", "AM produces higher-resolution images", "Training image search requires ground-truth labels; AM does not"], "c": 1, "e": "Training image search shows what real images look like including spurious co-occurrences. AM directly optimises the input to maximise a class score — revealing what the network actually learned to care about.", "tag": null}, {"p": "DeepDream differs from standard AM in two ways. Which answer correctly identifies both?", "o": ["DeepDream uses stronger TV regularisation and targets multiple classes simultaneously", "DeepDream targets an intermediate layer's activation magnitude rather than the final class output, and starts from a real image rather than noise", "DeepDream freezes earlier layers and only updates the last conv layer", "DeepDream uses the softmax output as a target rather than raw logits"], "c": 1, "e": "Standard AM: noise input, target = final class logit. DeepDream: real image input, target = intermediate layer activation magnitude.", "tag": null}, {"p": "Why should the raw logit (pre-softmax) be used as the AM target rather than the softmax probability?", "o": ["Logits are faster to compute", "Softmax is a competition — maximising p(C) via softmax encourages suppressing competing classes rather than genuinely building up class C features, giving muddier visualisations", "Softmax outputs are bounded between 0 and 1, making gradient computation unstable", "Logits retain spatial information that softmax discards"], "c": 1, "e": "Softmax normalises across all classes. Maximising p(C) can be achieved by driving competing class scores down rather than building class C features.", "tag": null}, {"p": "For GoogLeNet-based AM on ImageNet (1000 classes), the target vector y_target for \"flamingo\" (class 130) has what shape and values?", "o": ["Shape [224,224,3] — all zeros except the flamingo pixel region", "Shape [1000] — all zeros except index 130 which is 1.0", "Shape [1000] — all 0.001 except index 130 which is 0.999", "Shape [130] — a vector up to and including the flamingo class"], "c": 1, "e": "y_target is a one-hot vector of length 1000. For flamingo (class 130): all zeros except position 130 = 1.0.", "tag": "Dimensions"}]}, {"id": "c3", "ch": "l1416", "g": "l16-cnn", "ct": null, "cs": null, "sc": null, "tit": "Grad-CAM", "b": "L16 · §03", "q": [{"p": "For AlexNet conv5 output 13×13×256, after computing gradients and global average pooling, what is the shape of the importance weight tensor α^c_k?", "o": ["13×13×256", "256 — one scalar per channel", "13×13 — spatial map only", "1000 — one value per output class"], "c": 1, "e": "GAP collapses the 13×13 spatial dimensions to a single scalar for each of the 256 channels. Result: a 256-dimensional weight vector.", "tag": "Dimensions"}, {"p": "After computing ReLU(Σ_k α^c_k · A^k) for AlexNet conv5, what is the shape before and after upsampling?", "o": ["Before: 256 — After: 224×224", "Before: 13×13 — After: 224×224", "Before: 13×13×256 — After: 224×224×3", "Before: 13×13 — After: 13×13×3"], "c": 1, "e": "The weighted sum Σ_k α^c_k · A^k produces a 13×13 map (channels collapse). Bilinear upsampling stretches it to 224×224.", "tag": "Dimensions"}, {"p": "Why does Grad-CAM use the last convolutional layer rather than FC layers?", "o": ["FC layers have too many parameters", "FC layers collapse all spatial dimensions into a 1D vector, destroying location information; the last conv layer is the deepest layer still retaining a 2D spatial map", "The last conv layer has the highest resolution", "Conv layers produce normalised outputs; FC layers do not"], "c": 1, "e": "FC layers flatten the 3D tensor to 1D, losing spatial coordinates. The last conv layer is the deepest point where the representation still has 2D structure.", "tag": null}, {"p": "Why is global average pooling used to compute α^c_k rather than taking the maximum gradient?", "o": ["The maximum gradient is always zero", "Average captures each channel's overall contribution across the whole image; the maximum would overweight a single localised spike", "Max pooling cannot be applied to gradient tensors", "Global average pooling is differentiable; max pooling is not"], "c": 1, "e": "A single max-gradient position might reflect a localised artefact. Averaging across all 13×13 positions gives a more representative importance score per channel.", "tag": null}, {"p": "What is the purpose of the ReLU in Grad-CAM = ReLU(Σ_k α^c_k · A^k)?", "o": ["To introduce non-linearity", "To discard regions with negative weighted sums — regions that suppress the target class — keeping only regions that positively support the prediction", "To normalise heatmap values between 0 and 1", "To prevent the heatmap from highlighting the background"], "c": 1, "e": "Negative values indicate regions that work against the target class prediction. ReLU zeroes them out, leaving only positive evidence.", "tag": null}, {"p": "You run Grad-CAM on a dog+cat image. Re-running targeting \"cat\" produces:", "o": ["An identical heatmap — depends only on the image", "Grad-CAM cannot target a non-top prediction", "A heatmap shifted to highlight the cat half — different target class backpropagates different gradients, producing different channel importance weights", "No heatmap because cat probability is below 50%"], "c": 2, "e": "Grad-CAM is class-conditional. Different target classes produce different gradient flows, resulting in different α^c_k weights and therefore different heatmaps.", "tag": null}, {"p": "Key conceptual difference between Activation Maximisation and Grad-CAM?", "o": ["AM uses backprop; Grad-CAM does not", "AM synthesises what the network imagines a class looks like (class-level concept); Grad-CAM localises which regions drove a specific prediction on a specific image (decision-level explanation)", "AM works on classification; Grad-CAM only works on detection", "AM requires labelled data; Grad-CAM does not"], "c": 1, "e": "AM answers \"what does class C look like to this network?\" Grad-CAM answers \"why did the network predict class C for this particular image?\"", "tag": null}, {"p": "VGG-16 last conv outputs 14×14×512. Grad-CAM heatmap resolution before upsampling and number of importance weights?", "o": ["Heatmap: 224×224, weights: 3", "Heatmap: 14×14, weights: 512", "Heatmap: 7×7, weights: 512", "Heatmap: 14×14, weights: 1000"], "c": 1, "e": "The weighted sum produces a 14×14 heatmap. GAP of the 14×14×512 gradient tensor yields 512 importance weights.", "tag": "Dimensions"}]}, {"id": "c4", "ch": "l1416", "g": "l16-cnn", "ct": null, "cs": null, "sc": null, "tit": "Adversarial Images", "b": "L16 · §04", "q": [{"p": "The FGSM update is x' = x + ε · sign(∂L/∂x). What information does ∂L/∂x provide?", "o": ["How much each network weight contributes to the classification error", "For each pixel, the direction in which nudging it increases the loss — i.e. hurts the prediction most", "The probability distribution over all output classes", "The spatial regions most important for the correct classification"], "c": 1, "e": "∂L/∂x is the gradient of the loss w.r.t. the input image. Each element says: if I increase this pixel, does the loss go up?", "tag": null}, {"p": "Why does FGSM use sign(∂L/∂x) rather than the raw gradient values?", "o": ["The sign is cheaper to compute", "Raw gradients would concentrate large changes on a few pixels, making the perturbation visible; sign normalises every pixel to the same step ε, spreading damage uniformly and keeping it imperceptible", "The sign ensures the perturbed image stays within a valid pixel range", "Raw gradients cause numerical instability"], "c": 1, "e": "Raw gradient magnitudes vary wildly. A few pixels would get huge updates (visible artefacts). Sign normalises each pixel to ±1, so every pixel is perturbed by exactly ε.", "tag": null}, {"p": "What is the trade-off when choosing ε in FGSM?", "o": ["Larger ε increases training speed but reduces attack success", "Larger ε makes the attack more likely to succeed but makes the perturbation visible; smaller ε stays imperceptible but may not cross the decision boundary", "Larger ε is needed for targeted attacks; smaller ε for untargeted", "ε only affects iterations required"], "c": 1, "e": "Too small ε: may not cross the decision boundary. Too large ε: perturbation becomes visible to humans.", "tag": null}, {"p": "How is generating an adversarial image mechanically the same as AM, and what are the two key differences?", "o": ["Both use TV regularisation; AM targets correct class, adversarial targets wrong class", "Both freeze weights and backpropagate a loss to input pixels. Differences: adversarial starts from a real correctly-classified image (not noise), and targets a wrong class label", "Both use gradient descent on the weights", "Both start from noise; adversarial attacks use a negative learning rate"], "c": 1, "e": "Same machinery: network frozen, gradient of loss backpropagated to pixels, pixels updated. AM: noise input, correct class. Adversarial: real image input, wrong class.", "tag": null}, {"p": "A digitally crafted adversarial sticker fools a CNN on-screen but fails when printed on a stop sign at distances over 2m. Why?", "o": ["The CNN in the car has different weights", "The perturbation relies on exact pixel values that are degraded by printing, lighting, viewing angle, camera noise, and resolution loss at distance — any of which can destroy the fragile signal", "Stop signs are too small for the perturbation to remain effective", "Physical attacks only work in controlled lighting"], "c": 1, "e": "Digital adversarial perturbations are optimised for exact pixel values. Printing, lighting, noise, perspective — any of these can break the precise signal.", "tag": null}, {"p": "What is the crucial difference between adversarial perturbations and standard augmentation?", "o": ["Adversarial perturbations are applied to the test set; augmentation is only for training", "Standard augmentation applies random transformations; adversarial perturbations are worst-case — specifically computed via gradient to maximally hurt the current model's prediction", "Adversarial training changes the labels; augmentation preserves them", "Standard augmentation is imperceptible; adversarial perturbations are always visible"], "c": 1, "e": "Random augmentation applies arbitrary transformations. Adversarial perturbations are the single most damaging perturbation the gradient can find at this training step — worst-case data augmentation.", "tag": null}, {"p": "You apply FGSM to a 224×224×3 image. Shape of ∂L/∂x and resulting adversarial image x'?", "o": ["Gradient: 1000 — x': 224×224×3", "Gradient: 224×224×3 — x': 224×224×3", "Gradient: 13×13×256 — x': 224×224×3", "Gradient: 224×224×3 — x': 224×224×1 (grayscale)"], "c": 1, "e": "∂L/∂x has the same shape as x: 224×224×3. After adding ε·sign(∂L/∂x), x' also has shape 224×224×3.", "tag": "Dimensions"}]}, {"id": "n4", "ch": "l1416", "g": "l16-applied", "ct": null, "cs": null, "sc": null, "tit": "L16 Applied Questions", "b": "L16 · Applied", "q": [{"p": "A 3-class row-normalised confusion matrix has diagonal [0.8, 0.7, 1.0] for cat, dog, hen. What is the mAP and the most confused class pair?", "o": ["mAP = 75%; most confused: cat → dog (10%)", "mAP ≈ 83.3%; most confused: dog → cat (30% of dog images classified as cat)", "mAP = 90%; most confused: cat → hen (10%)", "mAP = 70%; most confused: hen → dog"], "c": 1, "e": "mAP = (0.8 + 0.7 + 1.0)/3 ≈ 83.3%. The off-diagonal entry in row \"dog\", column \"cat\" = 0.3 — the largest off-diagonal, making dog→cat the most confused pair.", "tag": null}, {"p": "Why is a Total Variation (TV) regulariser needed when synthesising an image via Activation Maximisation?", "o": ["To speed up gradient descent by limiting the step size", "Without regularisation, pixels are updated independently and the optimiser converges to high-frequency noise that maximises the class score but looks nothing like a natural image; TV penalises spatial derivatives, enforcing smoothness", "To prevent pixel values from exceeding [0,255]", "To force the image to match the average colour statistics of the class"], "c": 1, "e": "The unconstrained optimisation problem has many solutions — most are adversarial-noise patterns. TV(x) penalises spatial discontinuities, steering the optimiser toward smooth, perceptually coherent images.", "tag": null}, {"p": "In the Fast Gradient Method x' = x + ε·sign(∇ₓL), what distinguishes a targeted attack from an untargeted attack?", "o": ["Targeted attacks are imperceptible; untargeted attacks are visible", "Targeted: sets the perturbation GT to a specific wrong class, pushing x into that class. Untargeted: moves the prediction away from the true class without specifying a destination", "Untargeted attacks always require a larger ε", "Both are identical in computation; the distinction is only in labelling"], "c": 1, "e": "Targeted attack: GT = one-hot of desired wrong class → perturbation pushes across that specific boundary. Untargeted: GT moves the true-class entry away → network predicts anything but the truth.", "tag": null}]}, {"id": "a14", "ch": "l17", "g": "l17-core", "ct": "Lecture 17", "cs": "FCNs, Up-Convolution & Encoder-Decoder", "sc": null, "tit": "Transposed Convolution (Up-Conv)", "b": "L17 · §14", "q": [{"p": "Up-convolution / transposed convolution is mathematically equivalent to:", "o": ["An ordinary convolution with a different padding", "The backward pass of a regular convolution: distributing each input value across many output positions using the kernel", "A 1×1 convolution", "Bilinear upsampling"], "c": 1, "e": "The gradient of a conv layer w.r.t. its input is a transposed convolution. Each input scalar is multiplied by the kernel and scattered to multiple output positions — the exact reverse of regular conv aggregation.", "tag": null}, {"p": "Up-convolution is also commonly referred to as:", "o": ["Fractional-stride convolution OR \"deconvolution\" (a misnomer)", "Inverse convolution", "Reverse pooling", "Convolutional dropout"], "c": 0, "e": "\"Fractional-stride convolution\" or \"deconvolution\" (technically a misnomer). \"Transposed convolution\" is the most technically precise term.", "tag": null}, {"p": "How does a stride-2 up-conv increase spatial resolution?", "o": ["It inserts zeros between input pixels, then convolves normally — output is roughly 2× the input size", "It computes a moving average over the input", "It uses bilinear interpolation", "It crops and pads"], "c": 0, "e": "Stride-2 transposed conv: insert a row/column of zeros between each pair of input positions (dilated grid), then apply the kernel. Output is approximately 2× the input spatial size.", "tag": null}, {"p": "Why is up-conv learnable while bilinear upsampling is not?", "o": ["Up-conv uses GPUs; bilinear does not", "Up-conv has a kernel of learned weights trained by backpropagation, whereas bilinear uses fixed interpolation coefficients", "Up-conv requires gradient descent", "Bilinear upsampling has no gradient"], "c": 1, "e": "Bilinear upsampling: fixed geometric interpolation, no parameters. Transposed conv: learned K×K kernel updated by gradient descent.", "tag": null}, {"p": "For a transposed conv with input W, kernel K, stride S, padding P, the output spatial size is approximately:", "o": ["⌊(W − K + 2P)/S⌋ + 1", "(W − 1) · S − 2P + K", "W · S", "W / S"], "c": 1, "e": "Transposed conv output: (W−1)·S − 2P + K. For W=13, K=4, S=2, P=0: 12·2 + 4 = 28.", "tag": null}, {"p": "Where in a U-Net architecture do you find up-convolutions?", "o": ["Only in the encoder path", "In the decoder path, alongside skip connections from the encoder", "Replacing every max-pool layer in the encoder", "Only at the final 1×1 conv"], "c": 1, "e": "U-Net's encoder path uses conv+pool (downsampling). The decoder path uses transposed convolutions (upsampling) and receives skip connections from corresponding encoder levels.", "tag": null}, {"p": "A common artefact of stride-2 up-convs that practitioners must mitigate:", "o": ["Vanishing gradients", "Checkerboard artefacts — uneven overlap of kernel applications", "Mode collapse", "Catastrophic forgetting"], "c": 1, "e": "Checkerboard artefacts arise from uneven kernel overlap — some output positions receive contributions from more kernel applications than others. Mitigation: resize-then-convolve, or K=2S kernel size.", "tag": null}]}, {"id": "a15", "ch": "l17", "g": "l17-core", "ct": null, "cs": null, "sc": null, "tit": "FCN for Semantic Segmentation", "b": "L17 · §15", "q": [{"p": "FCN stands for:", "o": ["Full Convolutional Network", "Fully Connected Network", "Fully Convolutional Network", "Feature Cascade Network"], "c": 2, "e": "\"Fully Convolutional Network\" (Long, Shelhamer, Darrell, CVPR 2015) — every layer is a convolution, enabling dense prediction at arbitrary input sizes.", "tag": null}, {"p": "The defining insight of the original FCN paper:", "o": ["Replace FC layers with 1×1 convolutions so the network outputs a coarse spatial map, then upsample to image resolution", "Use attention instead of convolution", "Train two CNNs in parallel", "Use only depth-wise convolutions"], "c": 0, "e": "A 1×1 conv on a feature map of shape H×W×C produces H×W×K class scores — a spatial map rather than a single vector. This converts any classification CNN into a dense predictor.", "tag": null}, {"p": "Replacing FC layers with 1×1 convolutions lets the network:", "o": ["Run on smaller GPUs", "Accept arbitrary input sizes and output a spatial map of class scores", "Use less memory", "Skip training"], "c": 1, "e": "FC layers require a fixed input size. 1×1 convs operate on each spatial position independently, working on any H×W input and outputting a spatial grid of class scores.", "tag": null}, {"p": "FCN-8s skip connections fuse:", "o": ["Two copies of the final layer", "Coarse semantic high-level features with fine-grained low-level features from earlier layers", "Predictions from two different models", "Forward and backward passes"], "c": 1, "e": "Deep layers have rich semantic content but poor spatial precision. Shallow layers have precise spatial information but low semantic content. Skip connections combine both.", "tag": null}, {"p": "For semantic segmentation with C classes and image size H × W, the network output shape is:", "o": ["C × 1", "H × W × C — one class score per pixel, then softmax across C", "1 × W", "H × C"], "c": 1, "e": "Output is H×W×C — a C-dimensional score vector at every pixel. Softmax over C gives per-pixel class probabilities.", "tag": null}, {"p": "What loss function is used for semantic segmentation?", "o": ["Per-pixel cross-entropy, summed over the spatial map", "Mean squared error of pixel coordinates", "Triplet loss", "Adversarial loss"], "c": 0, "e": "Per-pixel cross-entropy treats each pixel as an independent classification problem, summed or averaged over all H×W positions.", "tag": null}, {"p": "Why combine coarse high-level features with fine low-level features?", "o": ["Low-level features know what; high-level features know where", "High-level features have strong semantic class identity; low-level features preserve precise spatial localisation — fusing both gives accurate AND well-localised predictions", "They use less memory", "Required by backprop"], "c": 1, "e": "High-level features (deep layers): know what is in a region but lost spatial precision due to pooling. Low-level features: precise spatial detail. Combining gives the best of both.", "tag": null}]}, {"id": "n5", "ch": "l17", "g": "l17-applied", "ct": null, "cs": null, "sc": null, "tit": "L17 Applied Questions", "b": "L17 · Applied", "q": [{"p": "How does an up-convolutional (transposed convolution) layer differ from bilinear interpolation upsampling?", "o": ["Up-convolution reduces spatial resolution; bilinear interpolation increases it", "Both increase resolution by sliding a kernel, but bilinear uses a fixed interpolation kernel while up-convolution has learned weights trained by backpropagation — equivalent to a convolution with fractional input stride 1/f", "Up-convolution uses max-pool style windows; bilinear uses average-pool", "They are mathematically identical; only the framework implementation differs"], "c": 1, "e": "Bilinear upsampling: fixed geometric interpolation, no parameters. Transposed conv: learned K×K kernel, updated by gradient descent. The forward pass of a transposed conv equals the backward pass (gradient w.r.t. input) of a regular conv — hence \"transposed\".", "tag": null}, {"p": "How do skip connections in FlowNet (channel-wise concatenation) differ from those in a denoising autoencoder (element-wise addition)?", "o": ["Concatenation discards spatial detail; addition preserves it", "Concatenation appends encoder features as extra channels (doubling channel count), letting the decoder learn to combine fine detail and semantics. Addition adds encoder activations element-wise as a residual, keeping channel counts manageable", "Addition requires the encoder and decoder to have different channel counts", "Both are identical; only naming convention differs"], "c": 1, "e": "FlowNet: decoder input = [upsampled_decoder | encoder_feature], channels double, decoder learns a mixing weight for each channel. Autoencoder addition: y = F(x) + x_enc — residual connection, preserves channel count.", "tag": null}, {"p": "Why do residual skip connections (y = F(x) + x) allow training of much deeper networks?", "o": ["They reduce the number of parameters per block", "The block only needs to learn the residual F(x) = H(x) − x (deviation from identity), easier when the optimal mapping is close to identity; the identity path also provides a direct gradient highway mitigating vanishing gradients — enabling ResNet-152 with 3.57% top-5 error", "Skip connections replace conv layers with identity mappings in the lower half", "They act as automatic learning-rate schedulers"], "c": 1, "e": "Plain deep nets degrade in training accuracy because identity is hard to learn via stacked non-linear layers. Residual connections make identity the resting state — only small corrections needed. Gradients flow along the identity path, bypassing potential saturation.", "tag": null}]}, {"id": "a16", "ch": "l18", "g": "l18-core", "ct": "Lecture 18", "cs": "Image Stylisation & Neural Style Transfer", "sc": null, "tit": "Neural Style Transfer", "b": "L18 · §16", "q": [{"p": "Gatys et al. (CVPR 2016) — what does the algorithm optimise?", "o": ["The weights of a CNN", "The pixels of an output image, with the CNN held fixed", "A style classifier", "A GAN generator"], "c": 1, "e": "Like Activation Maximisation, the CNN weights are frozen. Gradient descent runs on the pixels of an initially-random output image, minimising a combined content+style loss.", "tag": null}, {"p": "Content loss is computed by comparing:", "o": ["Raw pixels of the two images", "Feature activations at a chosen deep conv layer between the generated and content images", "Image histograms", "Edge maps"], "c": 1, "e": "Content loss = ‖F(generated) − F(content)‖² where F is the feature map at a chosen deep layer (typically relu4_2 in VGG-19).", "tag": null}, {"p": "Style loss is computed using which statistic of the feature maps?", "o": ["Mean activation per channel", "The Gram matrix — channel-wise correlations of activations", "Variance per pixel", "Spatial gradients"], "c": 1, "e": "G[i,j] = dot product of the i-th and j-th channel activations (flattened spatially). It captures which features co-occur — texture/colour statistics — without any spatial location information.", "tag": null}, {"p": "Why does the Gram matrix capture style and not content?", "o": ["It's computed at random layers", "It pools statistics across all spatial positions, throwing away where features fire and keeping only which features co-occur", "It only uses one channel", "It's mathematically invariant to colour"], "c": 1, "e": "By summing over all spatial positions, the Gram matrix is spatially invariant. Content (object layout) requires spatial relationships, which are discarded. Gram = \"what features co-occur\", not \"where\".", "tag": null}, {"p": "The total loss in Gatys-style neural style transfer is:", "o": ["α · L_content + β · L_style", "L_content × L_style", "−L_content − L_style", "L_content / L_style"], "c": 0, "e": "Total loss = α·L_content + β·L_style. Typical β/α ≈ 10³–10⁶ because the Gram-matrix term has a much smaller absolute magnitude than the content term.", "tag": null}, {"p": "Style loss is typically computed at:", "o": ["A single deep layer only", "Multiple layers from shallow to deep — shallow captures texture/colour, deeper captures composition", "Only the first layer", "The softmax output"], "c": 1, "e": "Computing Gram matrices at multiple layers (conv1_1 through conv5_1 in VGG) captures style at multiple scales.", "tag": null}, {"p": "Vanilla Gatys-style optimisation is slow because:", "o": ["It requires retraining a CNN per image", "Each new (content, style) pair requires hundreds of gradient-descent steps on the image pixels — there's no learned \"styliser\" network", "VGG is not differentiable", "It requires very large GPUs"], "c": 1, "e": "For each new image pair, ~500 iterations of LBFGS/Adam run on the output pixels. There is no feed-forward styliser. Johnson et al. (2016) trained a fast FCN for real-time style transfer.", "tag": null}]}, {"id": "n6", "ch": "l18", "g": "l18-applied", "ct": null, "cs": null, "sc": null, "tit": "L18 Applied Questions", "b": "L18 · Applied", "q": [{"p": "Why is content captured at a late convolutional layer, while style is captured via Gram matrices at early-to-mid layers?", "o": ["Late layers are faster to compute; early layers are more memory-efficient", "Late layers carry semantic, appearance-invariant features (object identity); early-to-mid layers respond to local edges and textures. The Gram matrix discards spatial arrangement, capturing feature co-occurrence statistics that define style without encoding what or where objects are", "Gram matrices cannot be computed at late layers due to high channel count", "Content loss requires softmax probabilities; style loss requires raw conv responses"], "c": 1, "e": "A late layer (e.g. relu4_2) activates similarly for \"a dog\" regardless of painterly style — it encodes what. Early layers fire on local texture patterns. The Gram matrix G[i,j] = ⟨F_i,F_j⟩ summed over all spatial positions captures texture statistics (where is discarded).", "tag": null}, {"p": "Write the Neural Style transfer total loss for output image x given content source p, style source a, and balance constant k.", "o": ["L = ‖C(x) − C(a)‖² + k · ‖S(x) − S(p)‖²", "L = ‖C(x) − C(p)‖² + k · ‖S(x) − S(a)‖², where C(·) is a deep layer's activations and S(·) is the Gram matrix of style-layer activations", "L = k · ‖C(x) − C(p)‖² · ‖S(x) − S(a)‖²", "L = ‖C(x)‖² + k · ‖S(a)‖²"], "c": 1, "e": "Content term ‖C(x)−C(p)‖² measures how closely x matches content image p. Style term ‖S(x)−S(a)‖² is the Gram-matrix distance from style image a. k ≈ 10⁵ balances the two terms numerically.", "tag": null}, {"p": "What is one advantage and one disadvantage of a feed-forward FCN styliser (Johnson et al.) vs Activation Maximisation (Gatys et al.) for style transfer?", "o": ["FCN: higher output quality per style; Disadvantage: slower to run (~2 min per image)", "FCN: ~1000× faster (real-time, under a second per image); Disadvantage: requires a separately trained network per style — cannot generalise to arbitrary new styles at test time without retraining", "FCN: works with any arbitrary style at test time; Disadvantage: cannot run on standard GPUs", "FCN: lower GPU memory usage; Disadvantage: can only process square images"], "c": 1, "e": "Gatys AM: ~500 iterations per (content, style) pair — 1–2 minutes. Johnson FCN: single forward pass — under 1 second (Prisma-style apps). But each FCN encodes exactly one style; retraining needed for each new style. AdaIN later addressed this.", "tag": null}]}];
